rk in  and preserving our our watersheds uh and  the forest that we have  uh palau uh is fortunate that we have a  large landmass and not a very big  population and we we believe that we're  a blessed and it is our responsibility  to protect them and develop it  sustainably uh  not only for the palauans but for the  world  so you face huge challenges because of  climate change and adapting to it is  expensive so were you reassured by what  was agreed at cop26 for example wealthy  countries there for example promised to  double adaptation finance for vulnerable  countries by 2025. do you feel that  progress is really being made  well you know i i know that it's it's  difficult um to make change  and but you know every little step  counts  but you know i think we need to  encourage um  our friends that said we need to start  taking bigger steps  and uh cop 26 was definitely a ray of  hope  that i think we're going in the right  direction  and you know i want to  thank france for being the first country  this year  to  to have the one ocean um  conferences summit in in france and and  we're hoping to continue that on in  palau with our oceans in april uh 13th  and 14th and of course uh on to lisbon  and i think what what we want to build  on is those commitments that were made  we need to increase the work whether  it's in marine pollution and  in protecting our seas iuu and and also  uh just  uh sustainably managing our our oceans  and really producing a sustainable blue  economy those are really the initiatives  that we need to work on together  and and and our our larger friends that  are making a bigger impact on climate  change need to step it up  and also contribute to the climate  mitigation and climate adaptation  and uh  where we're  we would like to see more and i think  that's why it's important that we have  these conferences and continue to push  the momentum forward because if we don't  as my friends uh from the marshall  islands and they had had me represent  them at the high level  uh ambition high ambition panel at  glasgow  said you know if we don't  turn this around at this conference  we're going under  and why should any country or any island  disappear  uh because  we didn't make the choices the hard  choices uh  to turn the tide around  uh  and i you know it's the same as saving  lives we've made big sacrifices  uh all of us around the world to protect  ourselves from coven and i think that's  the same  drive and initiative that we need from  all countries if we're gonna address  climate change  and  you know the glasgow gave us hope but  there's still a lot of work to be done  okay so what specifically do you need to  see agreed and acted on at cop27  well we've talked about um  climate adaptation climate mitigation  we've seen those commitments  but  we need to see tangible  that they really come out to the  communities and they really impact lives  you know a lot of it is um i have to say  has been talk  uh and  and then they've made it difficult for  us to access those funds and sometimes  made it difficult because they say well  we're  you're you're kind of not a small  developing or you're no longer at least  developing so maybe you can't access it  so those kinds of things if we're really  out there to help and help with the  mitigation and the impacts  and  and adaptation we need to make those  that financing accessible easily  accessible  and so it can really uh  make the impacts that it's intended to  do  but at the same time  we need those commitments that say you  know  if  if we're going to meet 1.5  we need we need to cut those emissions  ideally in half by 2030. we cannot wait  till 2050.  we need to start taking boulder action  sooner  and you've joined the commission of  small island states cosis on climate  change and international law to bring a  case uh to the un uh international  tribunal for the law of the sea so tell  us what you're trying to achieve with  that action and why you feel it's  necessary to take it  well at times i think  as as small islands  we see the change happening  we we suffer it every day  and and i think many times the action  the response  prima from the larger nascents is is a  little bit um  is behind it's it's not it's it's not  happening fast enough  so really the intent is to raise that  awareness and  to make people understand that the  choices that you make  every day impact the lives of people  around the world and especially small  island states so make the right choices  you know uh  bringing that action is to encourage  people to change  to make those commitments and  and to make the right decisions  so  hopefully  by saying  the large polluters pay  they will take responsibility  you don't have to pay you don't have to  pay if you make if you make those  changes  but uh we need them to make those  commitments and stick to them and act  more aggressively  so  well  you know a study has has been done and  we  we  are the least contributors but are  received the biggest impact on climate  change  and our voices need to be heard  and this is one way to let the world  know the challenges that we face and  hopefully  the voices can be heard and change can  happen  do you feel listened to is it hard to  feel listened to when you're such a  small nation just 18 thousand people  uh yes  uh we're small  and it's hard to find us on the map  and  and uh  and i think sometimes that that's why  we're we're  but you know one of the things that um  uh that's so important by these these  conferences and and these events it's  allowed us to raise that awareness raise  that voice  and uh you know we appreciate uh sky  news taking the time to reach out to us  and give us the opportunity to share the  challenges that we have on our small  islands uh so that the world knows that  there's we should not ever  live in a world where  we would have to go to textbooks or  history books to learn about these  islands and those cultures and 